{
  "requirement": "TBV exposes configuration for its supplied Bedrock, OpenAI, and Ollama\nagents. The configuration selects a backend and carries its model, connection,\ncredential-selection, and model-option settings.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub enum AgentConfig {\n    Bedrock(BedrockConfig),\n    OpenAi(OpenAiConfig),\n    Ollama(OllamaConfig),\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<D: Domain> AgentSettings<D> for AgentConfig;\nimpl Clone for AgentConfig;\nimpl Debug for AgentConfig;\nimpl<'de> Deserialize<'de> for AgentConfig;\nimpl PartialEq for AgentConfig;\nimpl Serialize for AgentConfig;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.agent_config`.\n\n**What the requirement asks for**\n- An `AgentConfig` enum with `Bedrock`, `OpenAi` and `Ollama` variants.\n- The configuration selects a backend and carries its model, connection, credential-selection and model-option settings.\n- The enum is `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n- Trait impls: `AgentSettings<D>`, `Clone`, `Debug`, `Deserialize`, `PartialEq`, `Serialize`.\n\nThe detailed field and serialization behavior is refined by `agent_config.serialization` and `agent_config.settings`. Those refinements carry their own tests, so I did not reassess them here.\n\n**How the tests cover it**\n- **Backend selection and settings:** `config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[all_backends_preserve_nested_settings_and_credentials]` deserializes one JSON value per backend (bedrock, openai, ollama). Each carries a model, connection fields (region, profile, endpoint), credential selection (credentials, `api_key`, `api_key_env`) and nested options. It asserts that `to_value` reproduces the input exactly. It also round-trips through a string and asserts `assert_eq!(config, copy)`. This exercises `Serialize`, `Deserialize` and `PartialEq` on every variant, and `assert_eq!` needs `Debug`.\n- **Defaults, credential resolution and option validation:** `config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[option_defaults_and_credential_resolution_are_deterministic]` covers default endpoint and model, environment-variable and explicit credentials, and the default OpenAI options through `AgentConfig::options_json`. It also asserts that non-finite option values are rejected for the OpenAI and Ollama backends.\n- **Variants in use:** `providers.rs#mod[tests]/fn[supported_backend_settings_create_transports_without_sending_requests]` builds each of the three `AgentConfig` variants, including ones deserialized from JSON. It connects a provider from each and checks the model options take effect through `output_limit` (16384 for OpenAI, none for default Ollama, 128 for Ollama `num_predict`, 512 for Bedrock `maxTokens`).\n- **Unknown input:** the deserialization test in `config.rs` (`configuration_debug_hides_credentials`) asserts that an unknown field is rejected.\n- **`AgentSettings<D>`:** the impl in `config.rs#impl[AgentConfig/AgentSettings<D>]` is an empty marker impl. The configured-agent tests in `configured.rs` pass `AgentConfig` values through the agent-setup path, which the sealed `Settings` impl in `domain_tools.rs` accepts. That impl has no conditional logic, so it needs no separate test.\n- **`Clone`, `Debug`, `PartialEq`:** these are derives on `enum[AgentConfig]` with no custom logic. `Debug` and `PartialEq` are exercised by the `assert_eq!` calls above. The obligation is only that the impls exist, and the derive guarantees that.\n- **Public surface:** `tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks the exported inventory against rustdoc.\n\n**Unresolved point**\nNo test checks `#[non_exhaustive]` directly. It is a compile-time attribute with no runtime behavior to assert, so I do not count it as a gap.\n\nI found no plausible requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept.",
